Martin Read wrote:

> Like any game with a defined win-condition, there are two primary
goals.
> 1) Have fun. (Ed: Of course! It's a *game*.)
> 2) Win.

Not necessarily.

Having fun is part of any game played for entertainment purposes, so
I'm unsure of your reason for including that.  "Winning" Nethack, as I
said before, involves an arbitrary series of actions that has little to
do with Nethack's play.  Indeed, Nethack's current endgame is new as of
NH 3.1.  Standard Nethack play predates the act of winning as it is
currently implemented.  And the actual ascension proceedure, itself, is
mostly trivial, it's all the stuff that just happens to be standing in
the way of it that's the true game to Nethack.

> In its present incarnation, the scoring system doesn't work for me.
> It doesn't measure how much fun I've had, and it certainly doesn't
say
> anything about how I've won, although as I'm fairly experienced, I
tend
> to think a low winning score (and a low winning turn-count) gets one
> more bragging rights than a high winning score.  But really, the
score
> as it now stands only matters if you lose.

Score is not supposed to be a measure of fun, it's supposed to be a
measure of skill.

Low score counts can be as impressive as high scores, just as it can be
as interesting to play contrarian, try-to-lose chess as to play to win.
There's nothing wrong with playing that way.  But that falls under the
heading of those metagames people invent to squeeze more entertainment
out of Nethack than "normal" play, so I'm not sure that a "low score
table" would be as universally interesting as one for high scores.
